Loops in the Task Manager Program
When building a Task Manager, loops are essential for repeatedly interacting with the user, managing the list of tasks, and processing input/output. Below we will explain how different types of loops (while, for, and foreach) are used in the program, why they are chosen, and examples of their use.

While Loops
The while loop runs as long as a condition is true. It's perfect for situations where we want to continue running a block of code until a user chooses to stop or some condition changes.

In the Task Manager, a while loop is used to display the menu repeatedly until the user decides to exit:

csharp
Copy
Edit
bool exit = false;

while (!exit)
{
    Console.WriteLine("\n--- Task Manager ---");
    Console.WriteLine("1. Add Task");
    Console.WriteLine("2. Remove Task");
    Console.WriteLine("3. View Tasks");
    Console.WriteLine("4. Save Tasks");
    Console.WriteLine("5. Exit");
    Console.WriteLine("6. Sort Tasks by Due Date");
    Console.WriteLine("7. Sort Tasks by Priority");
    Console.WriteLine("8. Mark Task as Complete");

    Console.Write("Choose an option (1-8): ");
    string choice = Console.ReadLine();

    switch (choice)
    {
        case "1":
            // Add task logic
            break;
        case "5":
            exit = true; // User chooses to exit, loop ends
            break;
        // Other cases...
    }
}
Explanation:

The loop continues indefinitely until exit becomes true.

This means the menu will keep showing, letting the user select multiple commands.

If the user selects "5" to exit, exit is set to true, which stops the loop.

For Loops
The for loop is ideal when you know how many times you want to repeat something. For example, when removing a task by name, you want to loop through the task list (by index) to find the matching task.

csharp
Copy
Edit
for (int i = 0; i < tasks.Count; i++)
{
    if (tasks[i].Name.Equals(name, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
    {
        tasks.RemoveAt(i);
        Console.WriteLine($"Task \"{name}\" removed.");
        break; // Exit loop after removal
    }
}
Explanation:

We use a for loop because we want to iterate over each index of the tasks list.

As soon as we find the task with the matching name, we remove it and break out of the loop.

Using a for loop (with indexes) allows us to safely remove an item during iteration.

Foreach Loops
The foreach loop is the best choice when you want to iterate over each item in a collection without worrying about indexes. It’s commonly used for displaying tasks or loading tasks from a file.

Displaying tasks:
csharp
Copy
Edit
foreach (Task task in tasks)
{
    Console.WriteLine(task);
}
Explanation:

The loop runs once for each Task object in the tasks list.

Each task is printed without the need for manual indexing.

Loading tasks from a file:
csharp
Copy
Edit
foreach (string line in File.ReadAllLines(FilePath))
{
    Task task = Task.FromFileString(line);
    if (task != null)
    {
        tasks.Add(task);
    }
}
Explanation:

Each line in the file corresponds to one task.

The loop reads every line, converts it to a Task, and adds it to the list.

Summary of Loop Usage in Task Manager
Loop Type	Purpose	Example Usage
while	Repeat menu until user chooses to exit	Menu loop with exit condition
for	Loop a fixed number of times with index access	Removing a task by name (using index)
foreach	Iterate over collections without modifying them	Displaying tasks, loading tasks file

Full Example of Loop Usage in Task Manager
csharp
Copy
Edit
bool exit = false;

while (!exit)
{
    Console.WriteLine("\n--- Task Manager ---");
    Console.WriteLine("1. Add Task");
    Console.WriteLine("2. Remove Task");
    Console.WriteLine("3. View Tasks");
    Console.WriteLine("4. Save Tasks");
    Console.WriteLine("5. Exit");
    Console.Write("Choose an option (1-5): ");
    string choice = Console.ReadLine();

    switch (choice)
    {
        case "1":
            // Add task (not shown here)
            break;
        case "2":
            Console.Write("Enter task name to remove: ");
            string name = Console.ReadLine();

            // FOR loop to find and remove task by name
            bool removed = false;
            for (int i = 0; i < tasks.Count; i++)
            {
                if (tasks[i].Name.Equals(name, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
                {
                    tasks.RemoveAt(i);
                    Console.WriteLine($"Task \"{name}\" removed.");
                    removed = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (!removed)
                Console.WriteLine($"Task \"{name}\" not found.");
            break;
        case "3":
            // FOREACH loop to display all tasks
            foreach (Task task in tasks)
            {
                Console.WriteLine(task);
            }
            break;
        case "5":
            exit = true;
            break;
        default:
            Console.WriteLine("Invalid choice.");
            break;
    }
}

Console.WriteLine("Goodbye!");
Conclusion
Loops in the Task Manager program allow the software to:

Keep running until the user exits (while)

Iterate through tasks with control over indexes (for)

Easily display or load tasks from collections (foreach)

Understanding which loop to use and when is crucial for writing clean, readable, and effective code.
